I don't know how to reproduce this case but I suspect you get a pure SSL error while the underlying raw socket is still alive. If this happens, only `CO_FL_ERROR` flag is set on the connection. `CO_FL_SOCk_RD_SH` flag is not set. Thus, in the H1 multiplexer, the error will be detected. But without the shutdown, we will wait. However, on the connection side, because an error was reported, nothing more is performed.

To validate my theory, you can apply this patch on top of the 2.7.4. If it is possible of course :)

```diff
diff --git a/src/ssl_sock.c b/src/ssl_sock.c
index 9d23b4f91..b7adf81a7 100644
--- a/src/ssl_sock.c
+++ b/src/ssl_sock.c
@@ -6687,7 +6687,7 @@ static size_t ssl_sock_to_buf(struct connection *conn, void *xprt_ctx, struct bu
        goto leave;
 
  out_error:
-       conn->flags |= CO_FL_ERROR;
+       conn->flags |= CO_FL_ERROR | CO_FL_SOCK_RD_SH | CO_FL_SOCK_WR_SH;
        /* Clear openssl global errors stack */
        ssl_sock_dump_errors(conn, NULL);
        ERR_clear_error();
@@ -6848,7 +6848,7 @@ static size_t ssl_sock_from_buf(struct connection *conn, void *xprt_ctx, const s
        ssl_sock_dump_errors(conn, NULL);
        ERR_clear_error();
 
-       conn->flags |= CO_FL_ERROR;
+       conn->flags |= CO_FL_ERROR | CO_FL_SOCK_RD_SH | CO_FL_SOCK_WR_SH;
        goto leave;
 }
```

@participant6 is there a way to force the bug using a crafted http client? I am looking to apply your patch to our release process and would like to add a regression test that provokes the bug.
And yes, we have some **very** old clients, still speaking HTTP/1.0 without SNI etc. there's a good chance their TLS implementations are bugged. What's still confusing me is why this never showed in HAproxy 2.6.x before.
Have there been any changes to 2.7.x in the `ssl_sock.c` that would trigger the bug?
